Fellow Nethackers,

I'm creating a separate thread, mostly to cut off from the ongoing
flamewar in the one started by JG Miller.  I agree that anniversaries
are a good time for reflection so let's try to evaluate the current
situation of Nethack and its community and what can be done to make
things better (as opposed to concentrating on what's wrong with the
Devteam).

Let's assume for this discussion that 3.4.3 was the final release.  If
we want to pick up the development of Nethack and make a fork that
would obsolete the current vanilla, like Nethack did to Hack a few
decades ago, there are 2 ways we can go.

1. Work on the current codebase, adding new features and fixing
things, basically continuing what slashem and sporkhack had started.
2. Break Nethack into pieces and assemble it into a modern OS project,
not only code wise, but in general.  By that I mean community
oriented, with a bugtracker, repository, beta releases, etc.  Code
wise that would mean translating into a modern programming language
like C++, Java, Python, D, etc (most likely object-oriented).

The effort would have to be very strong in order to convince people to
stop playing 3.4.3 (which isn't a goal by itself, but would mean that
we have made a new, better Nethack).

Personally I'm in favor of 2., so I'd be interested if there are any
people who have ideas and would like to talk about creating a plan/
vision of some sort, stating the goals, etc (we can start a wiki).  I
want to know what you hackers think about it :-)

Michal.


